  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAn area of showers continues to move east-northeast across the \nHighway 20 corridor in northwest Iowa. Some patchy drizzle will\nbe left in its wake. Elsewhere, patchy drizzle will continue to\noccur as stratus remains in place across the area, though cloud\nceilings have risen a bit since this morning, making the \ndrizzle more patchy in nature. Showers look to develop along the\nMissouri River Valley and lift northward starting late this \nafternoon/early evening and continuing into the early overnight\nperiod as warm air advection increases across the area. An \nupper level wave will pivot into the area overnight as well, \nwhich will help increase the coverage of this activity. Plenty \nof moisture in place as PWATs increase to around 1.75+\", \nhighest generally near and east of I-29. On top of this, there \nwill may be some elevated instability around 300 J/kg that \ncould allow for isolated rumbles of thunder. This activity will \ngradually shift east-northeast through Saturday morning, with \nadditional patches of drizzle likely behind these showers. In \nterms of rainfall amounts through tomorrow morning, we look to \ngenerally see around 0.75-1.25\" near and east of I-29, with \nlesser amounts the farther west you go across the area. A few\nspots could see upwards of an 1.5-2\" (20-30% chance) if \nmultiple pockets of heavy rain move over one area. But with\ntraining convection not expected, flash flooding concerns remain\nvery low. \n\nWill have to watch Saturday afternoon and evening as a secondary \narea of mid level vorticity moves across the area. There may be\nenough daytime heating near and west of I-29 to allow for \naround 500-750+ J/kg of CAPE to develop. There is a warm layer \naround 700 mb that any rising parcels will have to fight against\nbefore a thunderstorm could develop, but the lift provided by \nthe upper wave may be enough to overcome it, especially closer\nto I-29 where this warm layer is a little weaker according to\nsoundings. Should it break the cap, a few thunderstorms may be \nable to develop. Severe weather is not expected as weak shear \nwill be in place. So lightning and brief heavy downpours would \nbe the main threat if any of this development were to occur \n(20% chance). Might be a better chance (30-40%) of this \ndevelopment over northwest Iowa Saturday evening as an 850 mb \nfront moves into the area. Slightly better shear around 20 kts \nwill be place at that time, so perhaps a more organized storm is\npossible, but still not enough for a severe weather concern.\n\nThis 850 mb front will bring a very brief period of northerly \nwinds overnight Saturday into Sunday, which may help thin the \nstratus a little bit. But then fog will likely develop as ample\nmoisture near and at the surface will be still be present. Any \nfog and stratus will linger into Sunday morning, but any stratus\nis looking more shallow than what we've seen compared to the \npast couple of days so drizzle appears unlikely. With the more \nshallow stratus in place, should be able to mix out some of it \nSunday afternoon, allowing for a few periods of sunshine and \nwarmer temperatures, with highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s. \nThis period of drier weather will likely be brief though, \nbecause our next storm system is set to move into the area \nMonday night into Tuesday. Latest LREF probabilities for this \ntime period shows around a 20-30% chance of at least an inch of \nrain mainly south of I-90.",
